There's something I think about from time to time WRT Nethack's user
interface in case of invisibility. Two issues actually.

The first one is if the player is invisible but has no see invisible
property. Then we don't see the player glyph, but (technically) we
see the cursor at the player's position. I am not sure that is the
Right Thing. Occasionally, say if the cursor is switched off by any
means (a system UI bug or whatever), you don't see anything at all,
and depending on the configured cursor shape (e.g. a left aligned
vertical bar) the displayed position is also irritating. Well, all
that can be discussed as an external issue that has to be fixed or
respectively configured at the operating system level. But, anyway;
wouldn't it be more consistent if the player position is then shown
as an 'I' (as done with monsters)? After all you feel your presence
(as you feel adjacent monsters) and certainly know where you are
actually standing.

The second issue are ghosts and the like. Per default you don't see
them by ESP while those are in yet unknown territory. But you can
configure the glyph to become something different than a blank, so
you can see them from distance and, in case of shades, are warned.
(Technically you can of course explicitly interrogate every ~1600
blank squares, but for practical purposes that's irrelevant.) With
tiles I suppose you always see ghost type monsters. So preventing
the glyph reconfiguration for ' ' seems technically inappropriate.
Though an option would be to define the 'mindless' flag for ghosts,
so that you can't spot them as easily when configuring them - as
often done - using an 'X' glyph. (Note: The point is not whether
ghosts are mindless or not. This is just an easy technical way to
implement the look and feel you get with the original blank "tile"
you see in case of ghosts. I am also aware there are people that
want to just see ghosts and shades instantly.)

